# Exclusions for syncing this checkout to a compute node.
#
#   rsync -av -e "ssh -T" --exclude-from=.rsync-exclude ./ USER@HOST:~/payne-zero/
#
# The -T matters: a host configured with "RequestTTY force" allocates a
# pseudo-terminal even for non-interactive commands, and the tty layer rewrites
# \n as \r\n, which corrupts rsync's binary protocol stream ("unexpected tag N",
# error code 12). See solver-in-the-loop-cluster.md.
#
# No -z: the payload is source text on a fast network, and the 6.9 GB where
# compression would matter is .npz, which is already compressed.
#
# Sizes are from the dev machine, 2026-08-05.

# 11 GB, almost entirely Git LFS objects. The working tree already holds the
# checked-out copies of those files, so syncing .git would transfer the same
# data a second time. Sync the working tree; use git only if you need history
# on the remote, and then clone it separately.
.git/

# 824 MB and platform-specific. Rebuild it on the remote (see
# solver-in-the-loop-cluster.md section 2).
.venv/

# Numba's compiled-kernel cache. Keyed by platform, Python version, and numba
# version, so a macOS cache is useless on a Linux node and a stale one can
# crash the solver. Let the remote build its own.
.cache/

# Results. Sync these in the other direction, deliberately, not as a side
# effect of pushing code.
runs/
results/
*.log

__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.DS_Store
tmp/
tutorial_output/

# 6.9 GB of hash-verified static catalogs. Excluded from the routine code sync
# because it never changes and re-scanning it wastes minutes on every push.
# Transfer it once, on its own:
#   rsync -av --size-only --partial --progress \
#         source_data_files/ USER@HOST:/scratch/payne-zero-data/
# then point the remote at it with PAYNE_ZERO_DATA_ROOT.
source_data_files/
